<<<chatGPT
The command "openssl s_client -connect" is a command-line tool that allows you to establish a secure SSL/TLS connection to a remote server and display the certificate information.

When you run the command "openssl s_client -connect", it initiates a client connection to a remote server specified by the hostname and port number. The "s_client" part of the command tells OpenSSL to act as an SSL/TLS client, while the "-connect" option specifies the server address and port to connect to.

Once the connection is established, the command will print out the SSL/TLS handshake messages and display the remote server's SSL/TLS certificate information, including the certificate chain, the subject and issuer of each certificate, and the expiration date of each certificate.

The "openssl s_client -connect" command can be useful for troubleshooting SSL/TLS connection issues, verifying the identity of a remote server, and checking the validity and expiration date of SSL/TLS certificates. It can also be used to test SSL/TLS servers for security vulnerabilities or misconfigurations.
